<header class="main-header">
  <div class="series-title">Likutay Halachos · Choshen Mishpat</div>
  <h1>Hilchos G'viyas Chov Min HaLikuchos V'Apotiki 2</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Laws of Collecting a Debt from Purchasers and the Apotiki — 2 (§§1–5) · Based on "Anochi Hashem Elokecha" — Likutay Moharan I:4 · The elevation of Malchus, mi'ut hay'rai'ach, birur, and hashavas ha'avot</div>
</header>

<p class="source" style="text-align:center; margin-bottom: 30px;">A prohibition of benefit releases from liens, just like sanctification of the body etc. See there in the Shulchan Aruch.</p>

<div class="section-header">§1</div>
<p>Based on what our holy Rabbeinu, <em>zichrono livrachah</em>, wrote in the teaching <span class="scripture">"Anochi Hashem Elokecha asher hotzaisicha mei'eretz Mitzrayim mibais avadim"</span> in Siman 4 — see there well — concerning the elevation of the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> [Kingship] to its root, in the aspect of <span class="aramaic">zarka d'izd'rikas la'asar d'isn'tilas misaman</span> [a throwing that is thrown to the place from which it was taken]. For the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ratzon</span> [will] — the aspect of the forms of the letters that are clothed within all of existence. And all the wills receive from the <span class="key-term">R'tzon Ein Sof</span> [the Will of the Infinite], which has no change etc.</p>
<p>And this is the aspect of the stripping away of physicality, in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"and the <span class="key-term">Chayos</span> ran forth and returned"</span> <span class="source">(Ezekiel 1:14)</span> — specifically — for Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> desires our service, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"and You desire praise from clods of earth"</span> etc. And when one returns from the <span class="key-term">bitul</span> [nullification], then the <span class="key-term">r'shimu</span> [impression] illumines — to know that <span class="scripture">Hashem Hu HaElokim</span>. And this is the aspect of complete knowledge etc.</p>
<p>And all this is through elevating the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> to its root, through <span class="key-term">Viduy d'varim</span> [confession of words] before a <span class="key-term">talmid chacham</span>. And through the rectification of all the bad character traits that come from the four elements etc. See there all this well. And in the end, the conclusion of the verse — this is the aspect of <span class="scripture">"from the house of slaves"</span> — which are the four elements, which are all beneath the sphere of the moon, which is called by the name <span class="key-term">eved</span> [servant]. As is found in the holy Zohar: <span class="scripture">"Behold, My servant shall prosper"</span> <span class="source">(Isaiah 52:13)</span> — <span class="aramaic">this is the moon</span> (see there in the Zohar, Parashas Vayeishev).</p>

<div class="section-header">§2</div>
<p>And behold, it is known to every understanding person that the borrower is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">mi'ut hay'rai'ach</span> [the diminishing of the moon] — from which comes the poverty that causes one to need to borrow from his fellow. As is brought in the Zohar: <span class="aramaic">in the blemish of the moon — poverty</span> etc. And therefore: <span class="scripture">"the borrower is a servant to the lender"</span> <span class="source">(Proverbs 22:7)</span> — this is the aspect of the moon, which is called <span class="key-term">eved</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And the essential matter is on account of the blemish of the four elements, through the blemish of bad character traits and desires that come from them — which are the aspect of the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil, from which is the essential blemish of the moon, through which comes poverty — in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"in sorrow you shall eat of it"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 3:17)</span> — that is, the lack of <span class="key-term">parnasah</span>, through which one must fall into the aspect of a servant, the aspect of <span class="scripture">"the borrower is a servant to the lender,"</span> as above.</p>
<p>For the essential nature of the servitude — why the moon, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, is called by the name <span class="key-term">eved</span>, as above — the essential reason is only on account of its blemish and diminution: that it is unable to ascend and be included in its root, in the aspect of <span class="aramaic">zarka d'izd'rikas</span> etc. For when the moon, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, ascends to its root — then the aspect of servitude is entirely nullified.</p>
<p>For the essential servitude is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">yeishus</span> [materiality/ego], which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Asiyah</span> [Action/Doing]. And when a person, <em>chas v'sholom</em>, overpowers the matter over the form — through his bad deeds, through the blemish of the character traits and desires of the four elements, which are called <span class="key-term">avadim</span>, for they are the aspect of <span class="key-term">Asiyah</span> and <span class="key-term">yeishus</span>, the aspect of servitude, as above — then he is, <em>chas v'sholom</em>, in the aspect of the blemish of the moon, the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> in its diminution. Because it is unable to ascend to its root and receive from there abundance and great goodness — because the matter and <span class="key-term">yeishus</span> have overpowered the wills that are clothed within them, which are the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, as above. And then, as it were, even the moon is called by the name <span class="key-term">eved</span>, as above — because the aspect of servitude, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">yeishus</span>, has overpowered it, as above. And then from the blemish of the moon comes poverty — through which one must borrow from his fellow. And therefore the borrower is called a servant, as above.</p>
<p>And in truth, this man who has come to this measure — that he is compelled because of his straits to borrow from his fellow — when his fellow deals kindly with him and lends to him, he performs through this a very, very great <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span>. As our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said <span class="source">(Sukkah 49b)</span>: <span class="key-term">g'milus chasadim</span> [acts of kindness] is greater than <span class="key-term">tz'dakah</span> etc. For one who lends to his fellow as an act of kindness performs through this a very great rectification, and does a great benefit for his fellow both materially and spiritually.</p>
<p>For this one who needs to borrow is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">mi'ut hay'rai'ach</span>, as above. And the one who helps him and lends to him — he completes and fills him from his blemish, in the aspect of <span class="key-term">miluy hay'rai'ach</span> [the filling of the moon]. And through this itself, it can be that he helps his fellow so that he can be entirely saved from the poverty — by filling his lack, which is the aspect of filling the blemish of the moon. For through this the <span class="key-term">chitzonim</span> [external forces] are unable to nurse from him so much, and they do not nurse his abundance. And through this he can go from poverty to wealth. For one who lends to his fellow — he rectifies through this the aspect of the blemish of the moon, through which the poverty is nullified.</p>
<p>And therefore a very great rectification is made through this when one lends to his fellow as an act of kindness — for he fills the blemish of the moon, which is the essential nature of our service, as is known. And therefore the Torah warned about this many times.</p>
<p>But also the borrower must be very careful to fulfill the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> of paying the creditor — to pay his fellow. And through this he elevates the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> to its root, as it were, in the aspect of <span class="aramaic">zarka d'izd'rikas la'asar d'isn'tilas misaman</span>. For one who lends to his fellow — through this he arouses, as it were, the <span class="key-term">shefa</span> [abundance] to flow to the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, so that it will be filled from its lack — so that it can overpower and clarify what it needs to clarify, so that it can ascend.</p>
<p>And afterwards, when the borrower returns and pays what he borrowed from his fellow — through this he fulfills the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> of paying the creditor. And through this <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span>, as it were, the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> returns and ascends to its root. For the lender is in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ratzon</span>, the aspect of a generous heart — and through this he arouses the Supernal <span class="key-term">Ratzon</span> to bestow upon the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, which is in poverty. And when [the borrower] returns and pays what he borrowed — it emerges that he returns and elevates the money, which is the aspect of wealth that descended from the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> — he returns it to its root. And through this, Above as well, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> returns to its root, to the Supernal <span class="key-term">Ratzon</span>.</p>
<p>And when the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> returns and ascends to its root, then it ascends with all the <span class="key-term">beirurim</span> [clarifications] that it clarified during the time that it was below, in the aspect of poverty. And the essential <span class="key-term">beirurim</span> were accomplished through the assistance that one provided, and through the act of kindness that one person performed with his fellow — through which they fill its blemish somewhat, and through this it has the power to clarify.</p>
<p>And this is the essential nature of the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> of lending. For this borrower who fell into poverty until he needs to borrow — all of this is because he does not have the power to clarify the sparks that pertain to the root of his soul, and they are held by them. And through this comes poverty. And therefore he needs someone who will strengthen his hand — as it is written: <span class="scripture">"And if your brother becomes poor, you shall strengthen him; do not take from him interest"</span> <span class="source">(Leviticus 25:35–36)</span> etc. For through strengthening his hand with a gracious loan, through this they complete him somewhat, and through this he has the power to clarify. And through this he can be saved from the poverty, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§3</div>
<p>And let us return to the aforementioned matter — the matter of <span class="key-term">k'dushas haguf</span> [sanctification of the body] that releases from liens, and likewise the prohibition of benefit, as above. For all the property of the borrower is liened to the lender — for they have come into the aspect of servitude. For all things are from the four elements, and all a person's property — which is his wealth — is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>. And since he was compelled to borrow, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mi'ut hay'rai'ach</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">eved</span>, as above — therefore all his property is in the aspect of servitude.</p>
<p>And therefore <span class="key-term">k'dushas haguf</span> releases from liens. For <span class="key-term">k'dushas haguf</span> is the aspect of the nullification of <span class="key-term">yeishus</span> and the body — for the thing is included in the exalted holiness. For all things in the world must be included in their root, in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Ayin</span> [Nothingness]. And when one consecrates the thing with <span class="key-term">k'dushas haguf</span>, then it is included from <span class="key-term">Yeish</span> [Something] to <span class="key-term">Ayin</span> — for a holy name is called upon it, and <span class="key-term">Kodesh</span> [holiness] is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Chochmah</span> [Wisdom], as is brought. And <span class="scripture">"Wisdom — from <span class="key-term">Ayin</span> it shall be found"</span> <span class="source">(Job 28:12)</span> — as Rabbeinu wrote there in the aforementioned teaching.</p>
<p>And therefore it releases from liens — for the servitude is entirely nullified. For the essential servitude is in the aspect of body and <span class="key-term">yeishus</span> — through overpowering, <em>chas v'sholom</em>, the matter over the form. But through <span class="key-term">k'dushas haguf</span>, where the body is included in holiness and ascends from <span class="key-term">Yeish</span> to <span class="key-term">Ayin</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Kodesh</span>, as above — then the servitude is entirely nullified, as above. Understand well.</p>

<div class="section-header">§4</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">isur hana'ah</span> [prohibition of benefit], which also releases from liens like <span class="key-term">k'dushas haguf</span>. For the essential nature of the lien — that the borrower's property is liened to the lender — this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">beirurim</span> [clarifications], for this is the essential purpose of the loan. For the borrower must clarify what he needs to clarify until he rectifies the aspect of <span class="key-term">mi'ut hay'rai'ach</span>. And through this he will go from poverty and pay his fellow.</p>
<p>And therefore all his property is liened to his fellow — for they are all liened to return and ascend to their root at the time when the time of their clarification is completed. And this is the aspect of the lien of the borrower's property to the lender — until he pays him, which is the aspect of the elevation of the <span class="key-term">beirurim</span> to their root, as above. (For the general principle is that <span class="aramaic">through an arousal from below, an arousal is stirred Above</span>.)</p>
<p>And therefore <span class="key-term">isur hana'ah</span> releases from liens, as above. For it is known that we do not have the power to clarify except through what the Torah has permitted. But the things that the Torah has forbidden to us — we do not have the power to clarify them and elevate them to their root. And especially things from which benefit is forbidden — where we have no hold on them at all, and even benefit from them is forbidden to us — because we have no power to clarify them at all.</p>
<p>And not only that, but even the Torah has placed in our hands that we can forbid upon ourselves anything we wish, even its benefit. For the <span class="key-term">birur</span> [clarification] is in the free will of man. And the whole world and its fullness, Hashem gave to human beings — and everything is in the free will of man: to clarify them and elevate them, or the opposite, <em>chas v'sholom</em>.</p>
<p>And therefore, when a person wants to forbid upon himself some thing, even its benefit — the ability is in his hands. For the thing is in his domain — to remove his hand and domain from any thing, so that it will be upon him like a prohibition. And then he does not clarify it.</p>
<p>And therefore, when he forbids the thing from benefit — automatically the lien is released. For the essential lien is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">birur</span>. And when he forbids it from benefit, there is no <span class="key-term">birur</span> in it, and the lien does not take effect upon it. For the essential lien upon the property is for the sake of paying the creditor — which is the aspect of the return of the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> to its root, as above, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">beirurim</span> — that all the clarifications ascend Above. And therefore, when he forbids it from benefit, there is no <span class="key-term">birur</span> in it. And therefore it releases from liens.</p>
<p>Just as <span class="key-term">k'dushas haguf</span> releases from liens — because it has already ascended Above to the aspect of <span class="key-term">Kodesh</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Ayin</span>, through the consecration, and no lien takes effect upon it — so too, conversely, <span class="key-term">isur hana'ah</span> releases from liens — because there is no <span class="key-term">birur</span> in it, since it is forbidden from benefit.</p>
<p>And the power is in the hand of the borrower to consecrate or to forbid from benefit and to release the lien — even though all his property is liened to the lender, especially when he made it an <span class="key-term">apotaiki</span>. Nevertheless, the borrower has the power for this. For the essential <span class="key-term">birur</span> of all things depends on the free will of man — specifically on his free will, as is known.</p>
<p>And the essential <span class="key-term">birur</span> is in the aspect of the borrower — for there is the essential <span class="key-term">birur</span>, in the aspect of <span class="key-term">mi'ut hay'rai'ach</span>, which is the aspect of poverty and borrowing. And therefore there is the essential free will. And therefore the free will was given to the borrower: to consecrate or to forbid — even though he acts not according to the law. Nevertheless, the free will is in his hand for this. Since there is the essential <span class="key-term">birur</span>, and the essential purpose of the loan was in order to help him so that he could clarify, as above. And the essential <span class="key-term">birur</span> depends specifically on free will. And therefore the free will is in the hand of the borrower for this, as above. Understand the matter well.</p>

<div class="section-header">§5</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of the returning of the pledge many times, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"You shall surely return it"</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 22:25; Deuteronomy 24:13)</span> — many times. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: Behold, your soul ascends every night and I do not withhold it on account of so many sins — so too, you shall surely return it.</p>
<p>For in truth, the returning of the pledge is literally the returning of the soul. For the money is the soul, as is brought in the words of Rabbeinu several times. And one must accept the pledge and return it, even many times.</p>
<p>For when the borrower pays his fellow, he returns the money — which is the soul — to its root. And through this he elevates the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> to its root, as above. But when the borrower is still poor and has nothing with which to pay, and has only his garment alone to give as a pledge — it emerges that when he gives him the pledge, it is as if he gives his very soul. For the soul is rooted in wealth, as Rabbeinu, <em>zichrono livrachah</em>, wrote <span class="source">(Siman 69)</span>. And this is the aspect of the departure of the soul.</p>
<p>But Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> desires our service etc. And therefore even when one merits the aspect of <span class="key-term">bitul</span>, the <span class="key-term">bitul</span> must be in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"and the <span class="key-term">Chayos</span> ran forth and returned."</span> As is explained in the aforementioned teaching well, see there. For one must not remain there at the time of the <span class="key-term">bitul</span> — until the time when Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> comes to take His deposit. For He, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, desires our service. For even though this is the essential purpose — to be included in one's root — nevertheless one must return to <span class="key-term">yeishus</span> and the body, in order to clarify more <span class="key-term">beirurim</span>. For all the days that the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> lives, he clarifies <span class="key-term">beirurim</span>.</p>
<p>In just the same way is the aspect of the returning of the pledge many times — this is the aspect of <span class="scripture">"and the <span class="key-term">Chayos</span> ran forth and returned."</span> For when afterwards he returns the pledge to the poor borrower, he then returns to him his vitality and his soul, and he is completed somewhat from his lack, and his soul has the power to clarify. And it can be that he will be able to exit the poverty entirely through this.</p>
<p>And so one must conduct himself with him many times — perhaps he can strengthen his hand, to support him from his fall, to raise him from the poverty — by filling his blemish each time.</p>
<p>And through this it is possible to answer the question of our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>: if so, why does he accept the pledge — since he needs to return it each time? But in truth, through accepting the pledge from the borrower — through this the thing returns and ascends to its root, as above. And therefore benefit can reach the borrower when afterwards he returns the pledge to him. For his soul is renewed — which is rooted in the money — because it ascended to its root and returned. In the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"They are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness"</span> <span class="source">(Lamentations 3:23)</span>.</p>
<p>For the returning of the pledge is literally the returning of the soul, as above — as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said: that the Holy One, blessed be He, said: "Behold, I return to you the soul" etc. And therefore, through accepting the pledge and returning it — from this is made the aspect of the renewal of the soul. And therefore he has more power in his hand to clarify, and he can exit the poverty.</p>
<p>For this is also the aspect of <span class="scripture">"and the <span class="key-term">Chayos</span> ran forth and returned,"</span> as above. For it is for this reason that one must ascend each time to one's root, in running forth and returning — in order to renew his soul and his <span class="key-term">n'shamah</span>, so that he will have more power in the service of Hashem — to clarify and elevate more and more sparks. Through renewing his <span class="key-term">n'shamah</span> each time, through the <span class="key-term">bitul</span> in running forth and returning.</p>
<p>Understand these words very, very well. And see there in the teaching "Anochi" all the words in their proper form well. This ending was written in somewhat abbreviated form. Understand the matter well.</p>

<div class="translator-summary">
<h3>Summary of G'viyas Chov Min HaLikuchos V'Apotiki 2 (§§1–5)</h3>
<p>§1: Introduces the foundational teaching — LM I:4 "Anochi." Ha'alas haMalchus l'shorshah = zarka d'izd'rikas. Malchus = ratzon = t'munos osiyos hamulbashim b'chol hayeishus. All r'tzonos receive from R'tzon Ein Sof. Hipashatus hagashmiyus = v'haChayos ratzo vashuv. Viduy d'varim lifnei talmid chacham + tikkun arba y'sodos. "Mibais avadim" = arba y'sodos = tachas galgal hay'rai'ach = eved (Zohar: "hinay yaskil avdi — da sihra").</p>
<p>§2: Loveh = mi'ut hay'rai'ach → aniyus → eved loveh l'ish malveh. P'gam arba y'sodos (midos v'ta'avos ra'os = eitz hada'as tov vara) → p'gimas hal'vanah → "b'itzavon tochalenah." When Malchus (l'vanah) ascends to its root → avdus nullified. Avdus = yeishus/Asiyah = chomer overpowers tzurah. Malveh l'chavayro = miluy p'gimas hay'rai'ach = tikkun gadol (g'dolah g'milus chasadim yoser min hatz'dakah, Sukkah 49b). Loveh who pays back = ma'aleh haMalchus l'shorshah (chozair hamamon = birur oleh l'ma'alah). "V'chi yamuch achicha v'hechezakta bo" (Vayikra 25:35) → strengthening his hand fills his lack → enables birur → exits poverty.</p>
<p>§3: K'dushas haguf mafkia midei shi'bud. All nicsei haloveh = avdus (arba y'sodos = Malchus b'mi'utah). K'dushas haguf = bitul hayeishus v'haguf → niklal miYeish l'Ayin. Kodesh = Chochmah, "v'haChochmah meiAyin timatzer" (Iyov 28:12). Therefore: avdus nullified → shi'bud released.</p>
<p>§4: Isur hana'ah mafkia midei shi'bud. Shi'bud = birur (borrower must clarify → pay → Malchus ascends). "B'is'arusa dil'sata is'ar l'eila." Ain banu ko'ach l'varair ela bamah shehitirah haTorah. Isurei hana'ah: no t'fisas yad → no birur → no shi'bud. Even man can forbid things upon himself (b'chirah = birur). Borrower has the power to consecrate/forbid and release shi'bud → because ikkar habirur sham (b'chinas loveh = mi'ut hay'rai'ach) → ikkar hab'chirah sham.</p>
<p>§5: Hashavas ha'avot kamah p'amim = "hasheiv tashiv." HaKadosh Baruch Hu: "nafsh'cha olah b'chol lailah v'aini m'akbah" → af atah hasheiv tashiv. Mamon = nefesh (LM I:69). Mashkon = histalkus hanefesh (ani who has only k'suso). But Hashem rotze ba'avodosainu → bitul must be ratzo vashuv (not remain in bitul). When mashkon returned → chiyus v'nefesh return → renewed birur power. Kashya of Chazal: why accept mashkon if must return? Answer: through accepting → thing ascends to root → when returned → nischadesh nafsho = "chadashim lab'karim rabah emunasecha" (Eichah 3:23). Hashavas hamashkon = hashavas hanefesh mamash. = Ratzo vashuv → m'chadeish nishmaso → ko'ach yoser l'varair ul'ha'alos nitzotzos.</p>
<p class="translator-note">Based on Likutay Moharan I:4 "Anochi" · G'viyas Chov Min HaLikuchos V'Apotiki 2 complete.</p>
